<!--Post: 7064--><!--Time: 1108163459-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7064"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:tbjostad@cox.net" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Toto<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Toto<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, February 11, 2005 - 3:10 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Haytrader, There are about 30 parochial schools in and around Wichita. &#40;this is bible belt country&#41;. Most of them are K-5th or 8th grade. Then kids either go to Kapaun-Mt. Carmel &#40;protestant&#41; or Bishop Carroll &#40;catholic&#41; or public HS.   <BR> <BR>Kapaun Mt. Carmel is certainly near his &#39;comfort zone&#39;.  So was Holy Cross Luthern &#40;K-8th grade&#41;, but it&#39;s moved way east now.  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7064-->
<!--Post: 7066--><!--Time: 1108164702-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7066"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:bmodule_244@hotmail.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Bmodule<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bmodule<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, February 11, 2005 - 3:31 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->He was dropping all kinds of mortuary hints that day:lillies, under your nose, no bones about it,grave joke,bored to death. He might have been &#34;stoned&#34; or mentioning those things in &#34;passing&#34; but it might be a &#34;dead end&#34;.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7066-->
<!--Post: 7106--><!--Time: 1108184400-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7106"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ztargate<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ztargate<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, February 11, 2005 - 9:00 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Did you wonder if the man in the police car WAS BTK? Haha! <BR> <BR>I doubt that he would visit graves, I would think serial killers don&#39;t do because they have no emotional ties to their victims. IMO. <BR> <BR><IMG SRC="http://www.catchbtk.com/discus/clipart/happy.gif" ALT=":-&#41;" BORDER=0><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7106-->
<!--Post: 7919--><!--Time: 1108746214-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7919"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:BTK_Catcher@cox.net" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Catcher<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Catcher<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, February 18, 2005 - 9:03 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->It depends on your definition of &#34;emotional ties&#34;.  I feel that BTK has very strong emotional connections to his victims.  No, they are not emotions that we relate with necessarily, but more along the lines of him controlling their destiny, etc.  I believe that he feels &#34;godlike&#34; in their demise, and that they served a higher purpose, all relative to his master plan.  The killings were of course quite selfish, in and of themselves, but the motivation could be layered.  There&#39;s just something telling me that he is very compulsive in certain aspects, despite his meticulousness, and precision in planning.  In the same way that he can&#39;t seem to resist playing this &#34;end game&#34;, I feel he can&#39;t resist standing over the remains of a victim, and gleaming in his role. <BR> <BR>At the same time, it&#39;s complicated by the preface of his ability to completely disappear from the radar for X number of years.  He is quite calculated in every move he makes, but IMHO, he still takes risks that bear fruitful results; visiting the remains of the dead, re-visiting the scenario is his head, could be quite fulfilling an experience for BTK.  We know that photographs are a stimulus for this nostalgic response, so why rule out gravesites? <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
